# Creating HTML Elements

**Elements** are the building blocks of HTML. Each element is part of the content or structure, like a heading, paragraph, or image.

## Element Syntax

The "standard" way to create HTML elements is with two **tags**: an **opening tag** and a **closing tag**:

- The **opening tag** is the name of the elements in angle brackets (`<tag>`) where `tag` is the type of element being created.
  - For example, the opening tag of a `p` (paragraph) element is `<p>`.
- The **closing tag** is the same as the opening tag but with a forward slash (`/`) before the tag name (`</tag>`).
  - For example, the closing tag of a `p` (paragraph) element is `</p>`.
- The **content** of the element is placed between the opening and closing tags.
  - The content can be any text, HTML, or other elements.

![A diagram showing the opening, content, and closing tags of an HTML element](images/element_illustration.png)

For example, to create a `p` element with the text content "Hello, World!", you would write:
```html
<p>Hello, World!</p>
```

Here are a few examples of HTML elements:

[A `p` element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/p) represents a paragraph of text:

```{html autorun}
<p>This is a paragraph.</p>
```

[An `h1` element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/Heading_Elements) represents a level-1 heading (usually the main heading of a page):

```{html autorun}
<h1>This is a level-1 heading.</h1>
```

### Anatomy of an Element

So an element consists of three parts: the opening tag, the content, and the closing tag.

Try creating a `strong` element with the text `"hello"` in the code cell below:

```{html}

```

```+solution
<strong>hello</strong>
```

```+test
assert('strong').should('exist').run('Could not find a strong element', 'Created a strong element')
assert('strong').should('have.text', 'hello').run('The strong element does not have the text "hello"', 'Created a strong element with the text "hello"')
```

### Capitalization

Elements can have any case (capitalization) but the convention is to use lowercase. For example, `<P>` and `<p>` are equivalent but `<p>` is preferred. The same applies to the closing tag: `</P>` and `</p>` are equivalent but `</p>` is preferred.

## Self-Closing Tags

Some elements do not have any content and are "self-closing" (the HTML specification calls them "void" elements). These elements do not require a closing tag. For example, the `<br>` element represents a line break and does not have any content. It is a self-closing tag:

```html
<br>
```

You will also see self-closing tags written with a trailing slash, like `<br />`. This style comes from an older standard called XHTML, which required the slash. In modern HTML, the slash is allowed but has no effect. `<br>` and `<br />` mean exactly the same thing, so you can use whichever style you prefer, as long as you are consistent.

In the code cell below, create an `<hr>` (horizontal rule) element. This element is a self-closing tag that creates a horizontal line on the page. You can use it to separate content or create a visual break in the page.

```{html}

```

```+solution
<hr>
```

```+test
assert('hr').should('exist').run('Could not find an hr element', 'Created an hr element')
```

## Exercises

1. Create a paragraph element (`p`) with the text "Hello, World!" and run the cell to see the result.

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <p>Hello, World!</p>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('p').should('exist').run('Could not find a p element', 'Created a p element')
   assert('p').should('have.text', 'Hello, World!').run('The p element does not have the text "Hello, World!"', 'Created a p element with the text "Hello, World!"')
   ```

2. Create a level-1 heading element (`h1`) with the text "My First Heading" and run the cell to see the result.

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <h1>My First Heading</h1>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('h1').should('exist').run('Could not find an h1 element', 'Created an h1 element')
   assert('h1').should('have.text', 'My First Heading').run('The h1 element does not have the text "My First Heading"', 'Created an h1 element with the text "My First Heading"')
   ```

3. Create a level-2 heading element (`h2`) with the text "My Second Heading" and run the cell to see the result.

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <h2>My Second Heading</h2>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('h2').should('exist').run('Could not find an h2 element', 'Created an h2 element')
   assert('h2').should('have.text', 'My Second Heading').run('The h2 element does not have the text "My Second Heading"', 'Created an h2 element with the text "My Second Heading"')
   ```

